/**
 * @page gpio_control GPIO control
 * 
 * @if DOX_NAV
 * @ref dev_config "Back to the list of SDR device configuration sub-topics" 
 * @endif
 * 
 * @section gpio_summary Summary
 * 
 * Some SDR devices support the use of GPIO pins. GPIO pin count depends on the SDR device model and device GW version. For more information, refer to the respective SDR device hardware data sheets.
 * 
 * @subsection gpio_pin_addressing Pin addressing 
 * 
 * The GPIO pin addressing order maters, because GPIO direction and status read/write functions operate on a batch of GPIO pins at once. A GPIO pin batch is considered to be a single byte, 
 * where each individual bit addresses a single GPIO pin. Multiple batches of GPIO pins are passed as an array of bytes. This allows the functions to operate on 8 GPIO pins at once. GPIO pins 
 * for SDR devices based on LMS7002M module are addressed in an ascending order starting from the LSB bit of the first GPIO array byte. Consequently, the GPIO0 pin is addressed as a bit 0 and 
 * GPIO7 is addressed as bit 7 in the first GPIO array byte. If a SDR device, has more than 8 GPIO pins, the addressing continues in an ascending order and the bits 0-7 of the next GPIO array byte 
 * will address GPIO pins 8-15 and etc.
 * 
 * @subsection gpio_directions Pin directions
 * 
 * To get the current GPIO pin directions, use @ref lime::SDRDevice::GPIODirRead(uint8_t*, size_t) "GPIODirRead()" function. 
 * To set the new GPIO pin directions, use @ref lime::SDRDevice::GPIODirWrite(const uint8_t*, size_t) "GPIODirWrite()" function. 
 * 
 * For SDR devices based on LMS7002M modules, GPIO pins support the following direction modes:
 * <ul>
 *    <li>`INPUT` - 0</li>
 *    <li>`OUTPUT` - 1</li>
 * </ul>
 * 
 * For example, the following array ``uint8_t GPIODirs[2] = {0b00001010, 0b00000011};`` bytes indicate that the GPIO pins 1, 3 and 8, 9 are set to ``OUTPUT`` direction mode, while the rest of the GPIO pins 
 * are set to ``INPUT`` direction mode.
 * 
 * @subsection gpio_rw Pin states
 * 
 * To get the current GPIO pin status, use @ref lime::SDRDevice::GPIORead(uint8_t*, size_t) "GPIORead()" function. 
 * To set the new GPIO pin status, use @ref lime::SDRDevice::GPIOWrite(const uint8_t*, size_t) "GPIOWrite()" function. 
 * 
 * For SDR devices based on LMS7002M modules, GPIO pins support the following states:
 * <ul>
 *    <li>``HIGH`` - 1</li>
 *    <li>``LOW`` - 0</li>
 * </ul>
 * 
 * For example, the following array ``uint8_t GPIOStates[2] = {0b01010001, 0b10100010};`` bytes indicate that the GPIO pins 0, 4, 6 and 9, 13, 15 pins are in a ``HIGH`` state, while the rest of the GPIO pins
 * are in a ``LOW`` state.
 * 
 * @subsection gpio_example Example code for GPIO pin control
 * 
 * Below is an example code used to control GPIO pins of a SDR device. Code is designed to show the use of GPIO pins for SDR devices that support atleast 8 GPIO pins.
 * The following example code reads the initial and updated GPIO pin directions and states, outputs the results to the command line.
 * 
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <iostream>
 * #include <vector>
 * #include <cinttypes>
 * #include <limesuiteng/DeviceRegistry.h>
 * #include <limesuiteng/SDRDevice.h>
 * #include <limesuiteng/DeviceHandle.h>
 * #include <limesuiteng/OpStatus.h>
 * 
 * int main()
 * {
 *    std::vector<lime::DeviceHandle> listOfDevices = lime::DeviceRegistry::enumerate();
 *    if(listOfDevices.empty())
 *    {
 *       std::cout << "No devices detected!\n";
 *       return 0;
 *    }
 * 
 *    lime::SDRDevice * device = lime::DeviceRegistry::makeDevice(listOfDevices.front());
 *    if(device == nullptr)
 *    {
 *       std::cout << "Failed to connect to SDR device\n";
 *       return 1;
 *    }
 * 
 *    uint8_t moduleIndex = 0;
 *    uint8_t ChA = 0;
 *    const size_t GPIOBatches = 1;
 *    uint8_t GPIODirs[GPIOBatches] = {};
 *    uint8_t GPIOStates[GPIOBatches] = {};
 * 
 *    std::cout << "GPIO directions and states of a reset SDR device:\n";
 *    device->GPIODirRead(GPIODirs, GPIOBatches);
 *    device->GPIORead(GPIOStates, GPIOBatches);
 *    for(int i = 0; i < 8; ++i)
 *       std::cout << "GPIO" << i << " - " << (GPIODirs[0] & (1 << i) ? "OUT\t" : "IN\t") 
 *       << (GPIOStates[0] & (1 << i) ? "HIGH\n" : "LOW\n");
 * 
 *    // Reseting all of the pin states
 *    GPIODirs[0]=0xFF;
 *    GPIOStates[0]=0;
 *    device->GPIODirWrite(GPIODirs, GPIOBatches);
 *    device->GPIOWrite(GPIOStates, GPIOBatches);
 * 
 *    //Setting all GPIOs 4-7 to output 
 *    GPIODirs[0]=0xF0;    // GPIOs 4-7 output mode, GPIOs 0-3 input mode
 *    GPIOStates[0]=0x51;  // Set the GPIOs 1 and 4-7 to HIGH state 
 *    device->GPIODirWrite(GPIODirs, GPIOBatches);
 *    device->GPIOWrite(GPIOStates, GPIOBatches);
 *    std::cout << "Updated GPIO directions and states:\n";
 *    GPIODirs[0]=0x00;
 *    GPIOStates[0]=0x00;
 *    device->GPIODirRead(GPIODirs, GPIOBatches);
 *    device->GPIORead(GPIOStates, GPIOBatches);
 *    for(int i = 0; i < 8; ++i)
 *       std::cout << "GPIO" << i << " - " << (GPIODirs[0] & (1 << i) ? "OUT\t" : "IN\t") 
 *       << (GPIOStates[0] & (1 << i) ? "HIGH\n" : "LOW\n");
 * 
 *    lime::DeviceRegistry::freeDevice(device);
 *    return 0;
 * }
 * @endcode
 * 
 * Below is an output of an example code above used to control LimeSDR USB device GPIO pins. A reset SDR device will always set the GPIO pins to the ``INPUT`` direction mode and the default pin 
 * state to ``HIGH``. Although the default pin states are set to ``HIGH``, the actual GPIO pins on the SDR device are set to a ``LOW`` state (0 V). Default GPIO pin state values on 
 * device reset depend on the device gateware (GW) default register intialization procedure. Different SDR device GW versions may set the default GPIO pin states to states that are different than 
 * observed in the output image below.
 * 
 * For the purpose of demonstration, code resets the directions and states to demonstrate, that a pin configured in ``INPUT`` direction mode cannot be set to ``HIGH`` state from the user space. 
 * GPIO pin reset configures all GPIO directions to ``OUTPUT`` mode and then toggles all GPIO pin states to a ``LOW`` state. Once the GPIO pins are reset, a new GPIO direction and state configuration
 * is set. Now the GPIO pins 4-7 are left in an ``OUTPUT`` direction mode, while the GPIO pins 0-3 are set to ``INPUT`` direction mode, by updating array ``GPIODirs[0] = 0xF0;``. Next, GPIO states are 
 * updated, where the GPIO pins 0, 4, 6 are set to ``HIGH`` state and GPIO pins 5, 7 are set to ``LOW`` state, by updating array ``GPIOStates[0] = 0x51;``. Updated GPIO pin states are read back from the
 * device. As expected the GPIO pins 4 and 6 are set to ``HIGH`` state. However, the GPIO pin 0 is left unchanged, since it is configured to an ``INPUT`` direction mode.
 * 
 * @image{inline} html dev_gpio_control.png
 * 
 * 
 */